<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polyperchon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 394–c. 303 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polyperchon was a Macedonian general who served under Philip II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. In 319 BCE the dying regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> named him guardian of the kings in preference to his own son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, setting off a civil war among Alexander's successors. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, he lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> to Cassander by 316 BCE and later held out for years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Polyperchon was a Macedonian Greek general whose father, Simmias, belonged to the aristocracy of Tymphaia in Upper Macedonia and was descended from the region's local kings. His brother Andromenes had four sons who became commanders under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Claudius Aelian reports a rumour that Polyperchon was a bandit in his youth, but the passage is among many unreliable claims about the early years of well-known figures. He served Philip II and then accompanied Alexander on his long journeys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Battle of Issus</a> in 333 BCE Polyperchon took command of a battalion of the Macedonian phalanx in place of Ptolemy, who had fallen, and he held it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> in 331 BCE. In 328 BCE he was left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> to prevent rebellion, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-hydaspes">Battle of the Hydaspes</a> in 326 BCE he guarded the base camp with Alcetas and Craterus.</p><p>In August 324 BCE Alexander sent Craterus home with 10,000 veterans to replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> as regent and made Polyperchon his deputy. Both were still in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> when Alexander died in June 323 BCE. Polyperchon then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> while Antipater and Craterus crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>, and he crushed an Aetolian force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> under Menon of Pharsalus.</p><p>In 319 BCE the aging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> named Polyperchon regent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> and made his own son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> chiliarch, second in command. Cassander, who had expected to succeed his father, refused the arrangement, and open hostility followed. Polyperchon allied with Eumenes and proclaimed the freedom of the Greek cities, but he suffered a defeat at Megalopolis in 317 BCE, his fleet was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, and Cassander took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 316 BCE and drove him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Polyperchon joined Alexander's mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. She led an army into Macedon and had King Philip murdered, but Cassander captured and killed her in 316 BCE and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a> and the boy king Alexander IV into custody.</p><p>Polyperchon withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, holding Corinth and Sicyon, and later put forward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, a reputed son of Alexander, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>. In 309 BCE he murdered the boy and reached a settlement in which Cassander recognised him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of the Peloponnese with equal authority. After Ptolemy seized cities there in 308 BCE he reconquered much of the peninsula by 304 BCE, but in 303 BCE Demetrius Poliorcetes reversed many of these gains. After that he disappears from the record, though a passage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> suggests he may have lived into the early 3rd century BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polyperchon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the regent Antipater died in 319 BCE, his son Cassander fought <b>Polyperchon</b> and Olympias for Macedon and won control by 316 BCE, executing Olympias.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li></ul></section>
